.TH HPSV 1 "August 2026" "hpsatviews 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hpsatviews \- High-performance generation of satellite visual products from GOES imagery

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hpsatviews
.RI <command> " " <anchor_file> " " [options]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hpsatviews
is a high-performance command-line tool for generating
visual products (views) from calibrated environmental satellite data,
with a primary focus on the GOES-R family.

The program makes an explicit distinction between:

.TP
.B Image
A numerical representation of calibrated satellite data (L1b/L2),
containing physical quantities such as radiance, brightness temperature,
or reflectance.

.TP
.B View
A visual representation derived from an image, normalized and quantized
for human interpretation.

.TP
.B Product
A view (or set of views) associated with a recognized meteorological
semantic, such as true color, air mass, ash detection, or day/night composites.

The tool is optimized for speed, geometric rigor, and reproducibility,
and is not intended to replace full GIS or physical analysis platforms.

.SH TEMPORAL MODEL
In HPSATVIEWS, the term
.B instant
is used instead of “timestamp”.

An instant represents the effective observation time of a satellite scene
and is defined by discrete temporal components
(year, Julian day, hour, minute, second).
This emphasizes its physical observational meaning rather than a generic
time marker.

.SH BASIC USAGE
The general invocation syntax is:

.EX
hpsatviews <command> <anchor_file> [options]
.EE

The
.B anchor_file
identifies the scene, its instant, satellite, and acquisition mode.
All required auxiliary bands are inferred automatically.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B gray
Generate a grayscale view from a single channel or linear combination of channels.

.TP
.B pseudocolor
Generate a color-mapped view from a single channel or linear combination.

.TP
.B rgb
Generate an RGB composite from multiple channels or expressions.

.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B --help
Display a concise help message.

.TP
.B --list-clips
List predefined geographic clips available to the system.

.SH COMMON OPTIONS
The following options are available for all commands.

.TP
.BI "-o, --out " file
Output filename. If omitted, a deterministic name is generated automatically
from scene metadata and applied operations, following the pattern
.BR hpsv_{SAT}[_{SECTOR}]_{TS}_{CH}[_{OPS}].{EXT} .
The same template markers are accepted by
.BR -o :
{SAT}, {SECTOR}, {TS}, {CH}, {YYYY}, {YY}, {MM}, {DD}, {hh}, {mm}, {ss}, {JJJ},
{OPS}, {PROD}.
.br
.BR {SAT} " satellite (G16, G19)."
.br
.BR {SECTOR} " scan sector: "
.BR fd ,
.BR conus ,
.BR m1 ,
or
.BR m2 .
.br
.BR {TS} " instant, " YYYYJJJhhmm .
.br
.BR {CH} " channel or band (C01, C02, etc.)."
.br
.BR {OPS} " operations performed (-h, --clahe, -s, -G)."
.br
.BR {PROD} " short mode name (e.g. truecolor, ash); overridden by -N/--name."

.TP
.B "-t, --geotiff"
Write output as GeoTIFF instead of PNG.

.TP
.BI "-c, --clip " value
Geographic clipping. May be a predefined key or explicit coordinates
(lon_min lat_max lon_max lat_min).

.TP
.B "-G, --geographics"
Reproject output to geographic coordinates (WGS84).

.TP
.B "-B, --both"
Save both the native (fixed-grid) and the reprojected output in a single
run. The reprojected file name gets a
.B _geo
suffix.

.TP
.B "-f, --full-res"
Use the native resolution of the finest channel as the reference grid
when combining channels of different native resolution: in any
.B rgb
mode, or with multi-channel
.B --expr
in
.B gray / pseudocolor
(see BAND ALGEBRA below).

.TP
.BI "-s, --scale " factor
Spatial scaling factor (integer). Values >1 upscale; negative values downscale.

.TP
.B "-a, --alpha"
Add an alpha channel for transparency in NoData regions.

.TP
.BI "-g, --gamma " value
Apply gamma correction (default: 1.0, disabled). In
.B rgb
mode accepts 3 semicolon-separated values
.IR v1 ; v2 ; v3
for the R, G, and B channels.

.TP
.B "-h, --histo"
Apply global histogram equalization. If contrast becomes oversaturated, use
.B --clahe
instead.

.TP
.B "--clahe"
Apply Contrast Limited Adaptive Histogram Equalization (CLAHE)
with default parameters (8,8,4.0).

.TP
.BI "--clahe-params " params
Apply CLAHE with explicit parameters:
.IR tiles_x , tiles_y , clip_limit .

.TP
.B "-j, --json"
Save metadata to a JSON sidecar file alongside the image.

.TP
.B --cog
For GeoTIFF output, build a full Cloud Optimized GeoTIFF with an overview
pyramid. The default is a fast, multi-threaded tiled GeoTIFF without overviews,
which is preferable when the file is an intermediate that gets cropped or
processed further (the overviews are the bulk of the write time and are not
reused downstream).

.TP
.B "-v, --verbose"
Enable verbose (DEBUG-level) logging.
.\" @CUDA_BEGIN@
.TP
.B --cuda
Use GPU (CUDA) kernels: true color (including Rayleigh), grayscale and
pseudocolor. Unsupported modes and options fall back to the CPU. Available only
in binaries built with
.BR "make CUDA=1" .
.\" @CUDA_END@

.SH COMMAND-SPECIFIC OPTIONS
.SS gray
.TP
.B "-i, --invert"
Invert grayscale values.

.SS pseudocolor
.TP
.BI "-p, --cpt " file
Apply a color palette (.cpt file).

.TP
.B "-i, --invert"
Invert value mapping.

.SS rgb
.TP
.BI "-m, --mode " mode
Composite mode. Supported values:
daynite (default), truecolor, night, airmass, ash, severestorm, so2, custom.

.TP
.B "--rayleigh"
Apply Rayleigh atmospheric correction, LUT-based (truecolor/daynite only).

.TP
.B "--ray-analytic"
Use the analytic Rayleigh correction model instead of LUTs.

.TP
.B "--stretch"
Piecewise linear contrast stretch (shadow boost).

.TP
.B "--sharpen"
Ratio sharpening (truecolor/daynite).

.TP
.B "-f, --full-res"
Use the native resolution of the finest channel as reference.

.TP
.BI "-T, --cloud-temp " kelvin
Classify pixels colder than this brightness temperature (Kelvin) as night,
regardless of solar geometry (daynite mode only).
.B 0
disables it (default); typical value:
.BR 230 .

.TP
.B "-l, --citylights"
Use a city-lights background image for the night/daynite composite.

.TP
.BI "-N, --name " label
Descriptive product name, written to the JSON and GeoTIFF metadata as
.IR product .
Also available as
.B {PROD}
in
.B -o
patterns. Accepts
.RI short : "Long description"
to set both independently (e.g.
.RI ndvi : "Vegetation Index (C05-C04/C05+C04)" ).
Useful with
.B --mode custom
to give the composite a meaningful name.

.B custom
mode uses band algebra for each channel: it requires
.B --expr
(see BAND ALGEBRA below), with the R, G, and B expressions separated by
semicolons.

.SH BAND ALGEBRA
HPSATVIEWS supports on-the-fly linear combinations of channels using
simple algebraic expressions.

.TP
.BI "-e, --expr " expr
Sum and difference of terms
.RI a * CH1 + b * CH2 ... .
Supports +, \-, numeric constants, and bands (C01\-C16).

.TP
.BI "--minmax " min , max
Optional range to adjust contrast (linear stretch).

For
.B gray
and
.B pseudocolor
a single expression is used:

.EX
--expr "2.0*C13-C15-200"
.EE

For
.B rgb
mode
.BR custom ,
three expressions (R;G;B) are required, separated by semicolons, optionally
paired with three comma-separated min,max ranges:

.EX
--expr "C13-C14; C13-C11; C13" --minmax "-5,5;-3,3;200,300"
.EE

.SH OUTPUT NAMING
If no output filename is specified, HPSATVIEWS generates a deterministic
name based on the scene instant, satellite, processing mode, and operations,
following the same pattern described under
.B -o
above:

.EX
hpsv_{SAT}[_{SECTOR}]_{TS}_{CH}[_{OPS}].{EXT}
.EE

Example, using an explicit
.B -o
pattern:

.EX
hpsv pseudocolor file.nc -o "{SAT}_{CLIP}.png" -c mexico
-> G16_mexico.png
.EE

.SH GEOMETRY AND GEOLOCATION
Views are generated using rigorous geometric formulations.
Direct pixel-to-pixel reprojection from geostationary projection
to a uniform latitude/longitude grid (WGS84) is employed.
Geographic clipping is optimized when possible by applying it
prior to reprojection.

.SH PERFORMANCE
HPSATVIEWS is implemented in C (C11) and parallelized using OpenMP.
The design prioritizes speed, memory efficiency, and scalability
on multi-core systems.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
Each optimization can be disabled at run time, without rebuilding, so that a new
host can be evaluated against the plain path. Set the variable to 1.
.TP
.B HPSV_NO_PINNED_UPLOAD
Use a pageable host-to-device transfer instead of pinning the host buffer.
Worth checking first on a new machine: registering a 470 MB buffer costs 0.010 s
on an A30 host but 0.048 s on a desktop RTX 5060 Ti, where it is a net loss.
.TP
.B HPSV_NO_DEVICE_HANDOFF
Re-upload the composite for the reprojection instead of reusing the copy that is
already resident on the device.
.TP
.B HPSV_NO_PREAD
Read HDF5 chunks with
.BR H5Dread_chunk ()
instead of parallel
.BR pread ().
.TP
.B HPSV_NO_MEM_ZEROCOPY
Copy the pixels into the in-memory GDAL dataset instead of wrapping the existing
interleaved buffer.
.TP
.B HPSV_DISABLE_FAST_READ
Read NetCDF variables with
.BR nc_get_var ()
instead of the parallel chunked reader.

.SH REQUIREMENTS
.TP
A C11-compatible compiler
.TP
NetCDF libraries
.TP
GDAL (for GeoTIFF output)
.TP
libpng
.TP
OpenMP

